# userdirs.manifest — config owned by mod_userdirs (standard home folders:
# xdg-user-dirs + the rename-on-language-change step, w-userdirs).
#
#   • /etc/xdg/user-dirs.defaults — WHICH folders exist and their English
#     names (translated per user by xdg-user-dirs). W's curated six; shadows
#     the package's copy, so it is W's vendor data: managed.
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/userdirs.conf + .schema — W's default for the one
#     setting (LOCALIZE) and WHO may decide it.
#   • /etc/w/userdirs.conf — the admin's DEVIATIONS. Class override: the
#     update never touches it; `w-reset userdirs` drops the deviations.
#   • /usr/share/w/userdirs/hooks.d/ — what other subsystems run after a folder
#     is renamed (a W-Pack whose config names a path under PICTURES, say).
#     The directory is W's, the drop-ins belong to whoever ships them.
#
# ~/.config/w/userdirs.conf is where the setting normally lands (user-scope),
# created on demand and with no vendor default — class user-conf: `w-reset
# userdirs` deletes it (its "default" IS absence).
#
# NOT tracked here — xdg-user-dirs' own per-user state, not W configuration:
#   • ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs   — where each folder is, written by
#     xdg-user-dirs-update; the user edits it through `xdg-user-dirs-update
#     --set` or their file manager, and W must never reset it: it can point
#     at folders full of files.
#   • ~/.config/user-dirs.locale — the language the folders were named in;
#     the anchor w-userdirs compares against. Resetting it would make the next
#     login believe nothing changed.
#
# Format: <path>\t<class>   class: managed | override | user-conf
/etc/xdg/user-dirs.defaults	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/userdirs.conf	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/userdirs.schema	managed
/usr/share/w/userdirs/hooks.d/	managed
/etc/w/userdirs.conf	override
.config/w/userdirs.conf	user-conf
